$DIR/main.p:9:26: p holds a view of a (a.New[int](), line 7), which was reclaimed by a.Reset() at line 10, but the call deferred at line 9 uses it when the function returns
    p looks at memory that a owns, without owning it: the view was made by
    a.New[int]() at line 7. A view of a local arena's memory may be used only
    until Reset or Release reclaims it. At line 10 a was reclaimed by a.Reset(),
    so the view may look at memory that was released, or at a value that now
    belongs to something else. The call deferred at line 9 keeps what p held
    there and runs when the function returns, after the deferred calls made
    after it (section 12.19).
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use p before line 10, or call a.Reset() only after the last use of p
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)
